  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA cold front moves through the area on Friday morning. Ahead of\nthe front, the environment is stable and convection is unlikely\nto build along the front. Recent CAMs indicate showers with the\naforementioned front will likely be more scattered with light \nrainfall accumulations. Behind the front, skies clear and \nquickly destabilize the environment during the mid-day. Forecast\nsoundings show an inverted-V sounding across the interior, with\nup to 300J of CAPE to accompany. This setup could allow for the\ndevelopment of a stray isolated low-top thunderstorm, with \nembedded gusty winds possible if storms develop. Friday's storm \nsetup looks similar, with CAMs continuing to suggest decent \nmixing conditions and an isolated storm threat.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nEven if the isolated storm threat does not come to fruition on \nThursday or Friday, low-level mixing could still allow for gusty\nwesterly winds and potentially some marginal impacts across the\ninterior. With gusts up to 30mph possible Thursday and Friday \nafternoon, some loose tree branches could come down and \nunrestricted items like empty garbage cans and tents could get \nblown away.\n\nThe airmass quickly warms through the weekend as flow turns \nsoutherly with some brief East Coast ridging developing. 850 mb \ntemperatures rise to around 15C by Sunday with southerly flow \narriving. If mixing can extend up to 925 mb, this would spell a mild \nday with surface temps reaching the 70s inland.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3 DESCRIPTION...\n\nQuite a few moving parts with remnant moisture from newly formed \ntropical cyclone Isaias which is expected to make landfall on the \nGulf Coast this weekend, then quickly lift northeast towards our \nregion by Sunday night-Monday in much weekend form. In fact, the \nonly real sensible impact locally is looking to be the potential for \na rainy period, which is overall a best case scenario as the region \nstill needs rain due to ongoing deficits. Right now the NBM mean is \nunder half an inch regionwide, but should note there is still quite \na large spread in the guidance, as some deterministic outcomes bring \nin a soaking rainfall with quite a juicy environment that would \nsupport efficient rainfall, while other outcomes suppress the system \nsouth of our region with high pressure and ridging over the region \nholding firm.\n\nThe bottom line is this aspect of the forecast is still low \nconfidence at this range, with impacts raining from just a \ncloudy/dry period to a period of soaking rainfall on the table. Do \nexpect the greater chances for rain to be across southern zones \nregardless of how it exactly plays out.\n\nSome vigorous northern stream energy with a positively tilted trough \nwill swing through in the later Monday-Tuesday timeframe. Right now \nit appears this feature will be separate, and not phase with the \nremnants of Isaias. A period of rain/unsettled conditions may \ncontinue with a cold frontal passage around this time, and cooler \ntemperatures arriving towards midweek.",
